  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front brings the return of heavy rain and \ngusty winds Monday.  \n\nAn anomalously deep upper trough digging into the Ohio Valley will \nbecome cutoff as the broader longwave trough axis works its way \nslowly east. Its trough axis will cross over southern New England \nMonday night into Tuesday ushering in a much quiet and drier period. \nBefore that, however, we'll be dealing with a very moist and dynamic \nsystem for the next 18-24 hours. This, as the shortwave and surface \nreflection pull copious amounts of moisture north into New England \non strengthening SW flow (PWATs 2+ inches) with a 40-55 kt LLJ \nstrengthening as it's core moves over the Cape and islands. \nFortunately, even though BUFKIT soundings show 55kts down to about \n925mb, the low level inversion should keep the worst aloft. \nRegardless, it will be a gusty day, especially over southeast MA. \nWhile some brief stronger gusts can't be ruled out, they are more \nthan likely to top out around 40 mph on Cape Cod. \n\nRegardless of its impact at the surface, said LLJ will certainly act \nas a strong forcing mechanism for our heavy rain. This together with \ndivergence aloft/placement beneath the RRQ of an upper jet will lead \nto broad, deep layer ascent and widespread rain showers and \nthunderstorms. Continuing to expect an initial round of moderate to \nheavy rain showers this morning into the afternoon which has already \nbegun to materialize. The atmosphere is primed for some heavy \ndownpours and while we can't nail down exactly who will see these \ndownpours, any convection would lead to a better chance of those \nhigher rain rates hinted at by the HREF (low probability of as much \nas 1+/hr). \n\nThere's unlikely to be a real break in the showers and embedded \nthunderstorms but any pockets of clearing skies between showers may \nlead to increased instability parameters and a better chance for one \nof these thunderstorms to become severe as a final source of forcing \narrives in the evening in the form of a cold front. Any storms that \ndo get going will be in an environment with 30-45 kts of 0-6km bulk \nshear meaning potential for organization and some damaging wind \npotential. \n\nWhen all is said and done (ending in western MA by 8pm, eastern MA \nby midnight) a widespread 0.5 to 1.5 inches of rain is expected. \nThat being said, convective enhancement and potential for training \nthanks to boundary-parallel mean flow mean that localized areas \ncould see significantly more, on the order of 3-4 inches. This \namount, especially if it falls in a short period, may lead to street \nflooding and even isolated flash flooding situations. However, we \ndon't expect a widespread flash flooding risk with this system. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...After a quiet Tuesday unsettled weather returns mid \nweek through the weekend with periods of showers and storms.  \n\nAfter a beautiful, mostly sunny, less humid day on Tuesday under \nhigh pressure ridging, unsettled weather returns Wednesday into the \nweekend. A broad mid level trough sets up over the eastern U.S. with \nseveral shortwaves rotating through the base of the broader trough \nand this will bring periodic rain and thunderstorm chances each day, \nespecially in the afternoon/evening.",
